        "title": "Continuum Mechanics and Governing Equations",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Fluid dynamics is founded on the continuum hypothesis: treat the fluid as a continuous medium, ignoring molecular structure. This is valid when the Knudsen number Kn = λ/L (mean free path / characteristic length) is small, which holds for virtually all engineering flows. The governing equations are conservation laws — mass, momentum, energy — written in differential form. The material derivative is the key operator that connects spatial (Eulerian) descriptions to particle-following (Lagrangian) perspectives."
    }
